Part A — Client Brief
SmartCare currently relies on spreadsheets and paper records. Staff report:
- duplicate bookings
- difficulty locating patient information
- inconsistent appointment status
- limited appointment history

Management wants a small, maintainable system that supports:
- patient management
- practitioner management
- appointment management

Part B — Stakeholders & Scope
Stakeholders:
| Stakeholder          | Need                                               | Evidence                                                |
|----------------------|----------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|
| Clinic management    | Reliable reports, reduced errors, simple system    | Operational problems + reporting issues                 |
| Reception staff      | Easy booking, cancellation, record access          | Duplicate bookings, manual cancellations, lost records  |
| Practitioners (GPs)  | Clear schedule visibility                          | Limited visibility of availability                      |
| Patients             | Reliable appointments, fewer admin mistakes        | Impact implied by duplicate bookings                    |
| IT/system maintainer | Simple, maintainable, secure system                | Iterative development + small clinic scope              |
In Scope:
- Patient record creation and search
- Practitioner profiles and availability
- Appointment booking, cancellation, status updates
- Appointment history
- Basic operational reporting
Out of Scope:
- Billing or payments
- SMS/email reminders
- Integration with external medical systems
- Clinical notes or diagnosis storage

Provisional: Confirmation required
- Practitioner ability to edit their own availability
- Additional appointment status categories
- Emergency contact fields for patients

Part C — Functional Requirements:
FR-01: System shall allow creation of new patient records.
FR-02: System shall allow searching and retrieving patient records.
FR-03: System shall allow creation and update of practitioner profiles.
FR-04: System shall allow setting practitioner availability.
FR-05: System shall allow booking appointments for patients with practitioners.
FR-06: System shall prevent double-booking of practitioners.
FR-07: System shall allow cancellation of appointments with recorded reason.
FR-08: System shall allow updating appointment status (booked, cancelled, completed).
FR-09: System shall display practitioner schedules in a calendar view.
FR-10: System shall maintain appointment history for each patient.
FR-11: System shall generate basic operational reports.
FR-12: System shall support user roles with access control.

Part D — Non‑Functional Requirements:
NFR-01: System shall provide an intuitive and easy-to-use interface.
NFR-02: System shall respond to common operations within 2 seconds.
NFR-03: System shall ensure secure storage of patient and practitioner data.
NFR-04: System shall be reliable with no data loss during normal operation.
NFR-05: System shall support incremental enhancements without major redesign.
NFR-06: System shall support backup and recovery procedures.

Part E — User Stories & Acceptance Criteria:
User Stories:
US-01: As a receptionist, I want to create patient records, so that I can store their information accurately.
US-02: As a receptionist, I want to book appointments, so that I can schedule consultations efficiently.
US-03: As a receptionist, I want the system to prevent double-booking, so that I avoid appointment conflicts.
US-04: As a practitioner, I want to view my schedule, so that I can plan my day.
US-05: As a clinic manager, I want to generate reports, so that I can monitor clinic performance.
US-06: As a clinic manager, I want user access control, so that sensitive data is protected.


Acceptance Criteria:
AC-01 — Prevent duplicate bookings -
GIVEN a practitioner already has an appointment at a specific time
WHEN the receptionist attempts to book another appointment at that same time
THEN the system shall display an error and prevent the booking

AC-02 — Practitioner schedule view -
GIVEN a practitioner is logged in
WHEN they open their schedule
THEN the system shall display all appointments with times, patients, and statuses

AC-03 — Negative scenario: invalid patient search - 
GIVEN a receptionist enters a patient name that does not exist
WHEN they perform a search
THEN the system shall display “No matching records found”

Part F — AI Requirements Review (AI ON)
AI Reviewer Output - Summary
 - Suggested clarifying appointment status categories → Assumption, needs validation
 - Suggested adding user roles → Evidence-based (privacy concerns)
 - Suggested confirming practitioner availability editing → Assumption, needs validation
 - Suggested adding reporting requirement → Evidence-based (case study mentions reporting issues)
 - Suggested performance requirement → Assumption, but reasonable

 Part G — Verification of AI Review:
 - AI Suggestion: Add user roles
Decision: Accepted
Evidence: Sensitive patient data requires access control.
 - AI Suggestion: Add reporting requirement
Decision: Accepted
Evidence: Case study explicitly mentions reporting problems.
 - AI Suggestion: Add performance requirement
Decision: Modified
Evidence: Not stated by client; kept minimal (2 seconds).
 - AI Suggestion: Practitioner editing availability
Decision: Unverified
Evidence: Client did not specify; needs confirmation.
 - AI Suggestion: More appointment status categories
Decision: Rejected
Evidence: No client request; avoid inventing requirements.

Part H — Final SmartCare v0.2 Package:
Includes:
- Stakeholder analysis
- Scope
- 12 functional requirements
- 6 non-functional requirements 
- 6 user stories
- Acceptance criteria
- Assumptions & open questions
- Verified AI review evidence

Reflection:
The Ai reviewer highlighted serveral areas that I initially overlooked, particiularly around ambiguity
and testability. It noticed that some requirements lacked measurable outcomes, such as performance
expectations and access control, which are important for a real system even if not explicitly stated.
However, the AI also attempted to introduce new features that were not part of the client brief, such 
as expanded appointment status categories and practitioner conrtolled availability. These suggestions
were overreaches because they were not supported by evidence from the case study. One reuqirement that 
changed after review was the inclusion of user roles and access control, which become necessary once 
privacy concerns were considered. This change strengthened the sepcification without inventing new 
functionality. Requirements must have evidence because they form a contract between the client and
the development team. Adding features without client validation leads to scope creep, wasted effort, 
and misaligned expectations. Evidence based reuqirements ensure that the system solves real problems
and remains mangageable for the small clinic environment.
